  <deck-lane at="10,30" label="Author"></deck-lane>
  <deck-node at="20,25" label="Write"></deck-node>
</deck-graph>`,
  },
  {
    tag: 'deck-kpi',
    parent: 'deck-kpi-grid',
    summary: 'One figure of a KPI grid · the value, what it measures, and an optional qualifier.',
    attrs: [
      { name: 'value', type: 'string', note: 'The figure itself' },
      { name: 'label', type: 'string', note: 'What the figure measures' },
      { name: 'note',  type: 'string', note: 'Qualifier under the label' },
      { name: 'tone',  type: 'default | accent | ok | warn | danger | muted' },
    ],
    slots: [{ name: '(default)', note: 'Supporting text under the figure' }],
    example: `<deck-kpi-grid cols="3" ruled>
  <deck-kpi value="0" label="CDNs" tone="ok">Runtime requests</deck-kpi>
  <deck-kpi value="20" label="slides"></deck-kpi>
</deck-kpi-grid>`,
  },
  {
    tag: 'deck-milestone',
    parent: 'deck-timeline',
    summary: 'One milestone of a timeline · a date, a label, and the body text under them.',
    attrs: [
      { name: 'date',  type: 'string', note: 'Free text · "Now", "Q3", a real date' },
      { name: 'label', type: 'string' },
      { name: 'note',  type: 'string', note: 'Qualifier under the label' },
      { name: 'tone',  type: 'default | accent | ok | warn | danger' },
    ],
    slots: [{ name: '(default)', note: 'The body text of the milestone' }],
    example: `<deck-timeline direction="row">
  <deck-milestone date="Now" label="Source" tone="accent">Write the brief</deck-milestone>
  <deck-milestone date="Next" label="Proof">Run the checks</deck-milestone>
</deck-timeline>`,
  },
];

// The catalogue counts its own arrays · a figure in the prose below can no
// longer disagree with the entries it introduces.
const layoutCount = layouts.length;
const atomCount = atoms.length;
const optionalModuleCount = optionalComponents.length;
const childCount = optionalChildren.length;
---

<Docs
  title="Component library"
  eyebrow="Reference"
  description={`Every <deck-*> element · attributes, slots, tokens, live preview. The default bundle has ${coreCount} core components; ${optInCount} optional elements are documented below.`}
>

<h2 id="mental-model">Mental model · which one do I need?</h2>
<p>The core elements you will meet first fit in five practical buckets; sub-elements and helpers follow in the catalogue. Start with the layout, then add only the building blocks the slide needs.</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Layouts (8)</strong> · the slide's overall shape ·
    <a href="#deck-cover"><code>deck-cover</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-section"><code>deck-section</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-feature"><code>deck-feature</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-split"><code>deck-split</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-feature-cards"><code>deck-feature-cards</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-takeaway"><code>deck-takeaway</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-photo"><code>deck-photo</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-bento"><code>deck-bento</code></a>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The five you'll always use</strong> · the talk-shaping blocks ·
    <a href="#deck-code"><code>deck-code</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-md"><code>deck-md</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-card"><code>deck-card</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-callout"><code>deck-callout</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-badge"><code>deck-badge</code></a>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Domain blocks (8)</strong> · reach for when the slide needs one ·
    <a href="#deck-stat"><code>deck-stat</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-metric"><code>deck-metric</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-tier-list"><code>deck-tier-list</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-step-list"><code>deck-step-list</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-shortcut"><code>deck-shortcut</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-mermaid"><code>deck-mermaid</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-punch"><code>deck-punch</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-kicker"><code>deck-kicker</code></a>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Composition helpers</strong> · plumbing for spacing ·
    <a href="#deck-stack"><code>deck-stack</code></a>,
    <a href="#deck-grid"><code>deck-grid</code></a>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Presenter helpers (3)</strong> · for the day of the talk ·
    <a href="#deck-notes"><code>deck-notes</code></a>,
    plus the lazy-loaded <code>deck-overview</code> (press <kbd>O</kbd>) and
    <code>deck-presenter</code> (press <kbd>P</kbd>).
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  New to Rikiki? Read the <a href="/docs/cheatsheet">5-component cheatsheet</a>
  first · don't try to learn the whole catalog before your first talk.
</p>


<p>
  This catalogue details <strong>{layoutCount} layouts</strong> and <strong>{atomCount} building blocks</strong> from the default bundle, then <strong>{optionalModuleCount} optional modules</strong> and the <strong>{childCount} child elements</strong> those modules define. Layouts give a slide
  its overall structure; the building blocks fill the slots. Every group
  follows the same design-token contract · override
  <code>--deck-&lt;tag&gt;-*</code> on the host to retheme a single instance,
  or globally on <code>:root</code>.
</p>

<h2 id="layouts">Layouts</h2>
<p>One per typical slide kind. Pick the layout that matches the slide's job.</p>

{layouts.map((c) => (
  <article class="comp">
    <h3 id={c.tag}><code>&lt;{c.tag}&gt;</code></h3>
    <p class="summary">{c.summary}</p>
    {c.tag === 'deck-bento' && (
      <aside class="bento-guide">
        <strong>Bento composition model</strong>
        <p>
          <code>deck-bento</code> defines the grid tracks. Use
          <code>deck-cell</code> for measured areas and
          <code>span="columns x rows"</code> to shape the composition. A cell is
          a size container, so fitted type responds to the cell rather than the
          full slide.
        </p>
        <p>
          Use <code>deck-point</code> when text should size the item. Points with
          the same child structure align their internal bands automatically.
          Explicit <code>rows</code>, <code>col</code>, <code>row</code>, or
          <code>span</code> values disable that automatic alignment where the
          composition needs exact placement.
        </p>
      </aside>
    )}
    {c.attrs && (
      <>
        <p class="block-head">Attributes</p>
        <table class="attrs">
          <thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {c.attrs.map((a) => (
              <tr>
                <td><code>{a.name}</code></td>
                <td><code>{a.type}</code></td>
                <td>{a.note ?? ''}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </>
    )}
    {c.slots && (
      <>
        <p class="block-head">Slots</p>
        <table class="attrs">
          <thead><tr><th>Slot</th><th>What goes in</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {c.slots.map((s) => (
              <tr>
                <td><code>{s.name}</code></td>
                <td>{s.note}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </>
    )}
    <p class="block-head">Example</p>
    <CodeBlock lang="html" code={c.example} />
    <ComponentPreview code={c.example} full={layoutTags.has(c.tag) || c.tag === 'deck-notes'} />
    {c.tokens && (
      <TokenReference tag={c.tag} tokens={c.tokens} />
    )}
  </article>
))}

<h2 id="atoms">Building blocks</h2>
<p>
  The building blocks you slot inside layouts, grouped by what they are for:
  first the ones that carry text, then the ones that carry data, then the ones
  that shape the slide, then the ones that embed code and diagrams.
</p>

{atoms.map((c) => (
  <article class="comp">
    <h3 id={c.tag}><code>&lt;{c.tag}&gt;</code></h3>
    <p class="summary">{c.summary}</p>
    {c.attrs && (
      <>
        <p class="block-head">Attributes</p>
        <table class="attrs">
          <thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {c.attrs.map((a) => (
              <tr>
                <td><code>{a.name}</code></td>
                <td><code>{a.type}</code></td>
                <td>{a.note ?? ''}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </>
    )}
    {c.slots && (
      <>
        <p class="block-head">Slots</p>
        <table class="attrs">
          <thead><tr><th>Slot</th><th>What goes in</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {c.slots.map((s) => (
              <tr>
                <td><code>{s.name}</code></td>
                <td>{s.note}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </>
    )}
    <p class="block-head">Example</p>
    <CodeBlock lang="html" code={c.example} />
    <ComponentPreview code={c.example} full={layoutTags.has(c.tag) || c.tag === 'deck-notes'} />
    {c.tokens && (
      <TokenReference tag={c.tag} tokens={c.tokens} />
    )}
  </article>
))}

<h2 id="optional">Optional modules</h2>
<p>
  These elements live in <code>src/extras</code>. They are compiled as separate
  modules and reach the browser only when a deck imports them, so a deck that
  does not use one pays nothing for it. Import the matching file from
  <code>rikiki/dist/</code> after the core runtime. Several modules also define
  child elements · those are documented under the module that defines them.
</p>
<table class="optional">
  <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Module</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    {optionalComponents.map((c) => (
      <tr>
        <td><code>&lt;{c.tag}&gt;</code></td>
        <td>{c.summary}</td>
        <td><code>{c.module}</code></td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </tbody>
</table>

{optionalComponents.map((c) => (
  <article class="comp optional-comp">
    <h3 id={c.tag}><code>&lt;{c.tag}&gt;</code></h3>
    <p class="summary">{c.summary}</p>
    <p class="block-head">Module</p>
    <p class="module-path"><code>rikiki/dist/{c.module}</code></p>
    <p class="block-head">Attributes</p>
    <table class="attrs">
      <thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Type or value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{parseItems(c.attrs).map((a) => <tr><td><code>{a.name}</code></td><td><code>{a.value}</code></td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="block-head">Slots and children</p>
    <table class="attrs">
      <thead><tr><th>Slot or child</th><th>What goes in</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{parseItems(c.slots).map((s) => <tr><td><code>{s.name}</code></td><td>{s.value}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="block-head">Example</p>
    <CodeBlock lang="html" code={c.example} />
    <ComponentPreview code={c.preview ?? c.example} module={c.module} full={c.previewFull} />
    <TokenReference tag={c.tag} tokens={c.tokens.split(' · ')} />

    {optionalChildren.filter((child) => child.parent === c.tag).map((child) => (
      <section class="child-comp">
        <h4 id={child.tag}><code>&lt;{child.tag}&gt;</code></h4>
        <p class="summary">{child.summary}</p>
        <p class="block-head">Attributes</p>
        <table class="attrs">
          <thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {(child.attrs ?? []).map((a) => (
              <tr>
                <td><code>{a.name}</code></td>
                <td><code>{a.type}</code></td>
                <td>{a.note ?? ''}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        {child.slots && (
          <>
            <p class="block-head">Slots</p>
            <table class="attrs">
              <thead><tr><th>Slot</th><th>What goes in</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {child.slots.map((slot) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>{slot.name}</code></td>
                    <td>{slot.note}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </>
        )}
        <p class="block-head">Example</p>
        <CodeBlock lang="html" code={child.example} />
      </section>
    ))}
  </article>
))}

<h2 id="next">Next</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="/docs/plugins">Optional modules</a> · what each module costs and how a deck imports it.</li>
  <li><a href="/docs/theming">Theming</a> · the token contract these components read.</li>
  <li><a href="/docs/api">API reference</a> · the deck-root properties, events and extension hooks.</li>
